Third Session
Policy Questions [Five items]

Question: The suburban sacrifices for making offerings to the Lord on High and the schools for honoring Confucian scholars—both of these are the foremost duties of governance for emperors and kings. Ever since the pure sacrifices and the burning of firewood on the altar, and the submission of hearts at the Biyong royal college, nothing could surpass the flourishing of the Yu and Zhou eras. Their institutional arrangements are fully recorded in the Rites of Zhou (Zhouli) and the various chapters of the Book of Rites by Dai the Elder (Da Dai ji) and its commentaries; can the ceremonial principles of their refinement and simplicity, and their additions and reductions, be recounted in detail? Rulers of the Han, Tang, and Song dynasties who desired good governance at times also admired and followed in their footsteps, making for fine tales in the histories. Yet the Han added the Northern Altar on account of the Black Emperor, the Tang sacrificed to the Supreme Subtlety (Taiwei) constellation at the Southern Suburb, and the Song placed the Lords on High of the Five Directions together and sacrificed to them—did these truly accord with the rites for making offerings to the Lord on High? In the Zhongyuan era [of the Later Han], the emperor visited the Biyong and paid respects to the aged; in the Zhenguan era [of the Tang], renowned Confucian scholars were summoned on a grand scale; and in the Qingli era [of the Song], the lecture halls of the Imperial University (Taixue) were expanded and renovated in preparation for imperial visits—did these truly correspond with the mind that honors Confucian scholars? Our Grand Progenitor (Taizu), with utmost sincerity reaching Heaven, esteemed civil culture and valued the Way. When the Precious Throne was first settled, he immediately paid careful attention to…
